-- title:
--   Existence of a third addition law covering each chart of E× E
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain and Noetherian, and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $R$ satisfying `W.IsElliptic`. Write $E =$ `projModelCR W.toProjective` for $\mathrm{Proj}$ of the grading on $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}(\mathrm{Fin}\,3, R)$ modulo the homogeneous ideal generated by the Weierstrass cubic of `W.toProjective`, obtained by pushing the homogeneous submodules forward along the quotient map; and for $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,3$ let $\mathcal A_i$ denote the degree-zero homogeneous localisation `HomogeneousLocalization.Away` of that graded quotient ring at the class of $X_i$. The assertion is that there exist a family of elements $u_3(i,j,k) \in \mathcal A_i \otimes_R \mathcal A_j$, indexed by $i,j,k \in \mathrm{Fin}\,3$, together with morphisms $\mathrm{toE}_3(i,j,k) \colon \mathrm{Spec}\bigl((\mathcal A_i \otimes_R \mathcal A_j)[1/u_3(i,j,k)]\bigr) \to E$, such that: (i) for all $i,j$ the ideal of $\mathcal A_i \otimes_R \mathcal A_j$ generated by the union of the range of `kw_lrSixU W i j` — the six elements given by `kw_lrChart_u` on the left summand and `kw_lrSymChart_u` on the right summand of $\mathrm{Fin}\,3 \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,3$ — and the range of $u_3(i,j,\cdot)$ is the whole ring; and (ii) for all $i,j,k$ and all $l \in \mathrm{Fin}\,3 \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,3$, on the scheme-theoretic pullback of the localisation map to $(\mathcal A_i \otimes_R \mathcal A_j)[1/u_3(i,j,k)]$ against `kw_lrSixU_locMap W i j l`, the first projection followed by $\mathrm{toE}_3(i,j,k)$ equals the second projection followed by `kw_lrSixU_toE W i j l`.
--
--   This packages, in existential form, the completion of the six chart-maps coming from the chord and symmetric bidegree-$(2,2)$ addition laws by a third law, so that the nine resulting open loci cover each of the nine standard affine charts $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal A_i \otimes_R \mathcal A_j)$ of $E \times_R E$ and all the chart-maps to $E$ agree on overlaps. It is the input to the construction of the relative group law on the smooth projective Weierstrass model, used in particular by [`WeierstrassProjModel.exists_relativeGroupLaw_isPointsEval_of_isElliptic_of_isDomain`](thm.html#WeierstrassProjModel.exists_relativeGroupLaw_isPointsEval_of_isElliptic_of_isDomain).
